<%#
  Modal host. Single shared shell for any modal that opens through
  Alpine.store('modals'). Owns the backdrop, scroll lock (via
  modal-open on body), escape key, click-outside, ARIA dialog role,
  and the mount and unmount animations. The behaviour behind it (the
  stack, directional swap() and advance() slides for multi-step flows,
  the focus trap, the per-modal animation registry
  window.ModalAnimations, bfcache and Turbo Drive snapshot cleanup,
  store registration, and the window.StudioModals.holdAtLeast(ms)
  helper) is studio/modal_host, bound to this host by the modal-host
  Stimulus controller on its template. This partial renders no script.
  The animation keyframes ship inline in the style block below, so
  consumers need no extra CSS to get the full behavior. A canonical
  copy of the SAME keyframes also lives in the shared motion layer
  (app/assets/tailwind/studio_engine/engine-motion.css) for pages that
  drive a modal host WITHOUT rendering this partial, e.g. the living
  style guide's page-scoped dsModals host. The two are byte-identical;
  keep them in sync if you retune a curve or duration.

  THREE CONSUMER EXTENSION POINTS, so an app never has to fork this file
  to change an animation, a width, or register an app-wide modal:

    window.ModalAnimations         per-modal enter/exit animation, merged
                                   over the engine defaults
                                   (studio/modal_host ANIMATION_DEFAULTS)
    window.StudioModals.CARD_WIDTHS  per-modal card width by modal id,
                                   merged the same way; DEFAULT_CARD_WIDTH
                                   covers every id it does not name
    app/views/modals/_host_extras  OPTIONAL app partial, rendered inside
                                   the card on every render path through
                                   this host, for a modal that belongs to
                                   the app rather than to one call site

  An app defines either registry in a script anywhere in the page (the
  module merges it once the document has parsed) and may change it later:
  the store reads both at call time.

  Consumer integration example and API reference: the "Modal host"
  section of the gem README, which also carries the two rules this host
  IMPOSES on the content partials registered in its block: a SINGLE ROOT
  element (a template x-if clones only the first one; later siblings are
  dropped) and NO double quote inside x-data (it closes the attribute and
  Alpine mounts a no-op). NEITHER STOPS THE RENDER, BUT ONLY ONE IS SILENT:
  the single-root miss logs nothing at all, while the truncated x-data prints
  "Alpine Expression Error" to the console (and rethrows it async). That
  warning is the clue rule one never gives you, so check the console before
  you re-read the markup.
%>
<style>
  /* Scroll lock applied by $store.modals._sync() (studio/modal_host) when the stack is non-empty.
     Combined with the fixed backdrop on the host below, this keeps the page
     beneath any modal from scrolling on wheel / touch / spacebar — a claim that
     was FALSE wherever this gem's link sidebar shipped, until the rule below
     moved off body. */
  /* THE SCROLL LOCK GOES ON THE ELEMENT THAT ACTUALLY SCROLLS.
     Measured on a consumer, 2026-08-27, before this change: with a modal open a
     real wheel gesture still scrolled the page (600px → 1000px) and the sticky
     header unpinned and slid away with it. The lock had been inert there for as
     long as the link sidebar had shipped.

     `body { overflow: hidden }` locks the viewport only when it PROPAGATES to
     it, and body's overflow propagates only while `html` is `overflow: visible`.
     This gem's own link-sidebar partial sets `html { overflow-x: clip }` so the
     off-canvas panel never flashes a scrollbar — correct on its own terms, clip
     creates no scroll container — but it ends the propagation. The lock then
     stopped locking the viewport and started making BODY a scroll container
     instead: one holding scrollTop 0 forever while the viewport scrolled past
     it. Every `position: sticky` child of body then has a scrollport that never
     moves, which is why the header unpinned and why the modal-open banner lift
     had nothing to pin against.

     So lock `html`, and put body back to `visible` so it is not a second scroll
     container. An app that sets no overflow on html is unaffected — locking html
     is correct there too; it was simply never the only thing that worked.

     :has() is the trigger because the class lands on body, not html. */
  html:has(body.modal-open) { overflow: hidden; }
  body.modal-open { overflow: visible; }

  /* Drain-bar keyframe used by studio/modals/blocks/_success_card when
     cta_drain is set — the CTA button hosts a translucent overlay that
     shrinks from full to empty over the auto-redirect duration. */
  @keyframes studio-modal-drain {
    from { transform: scaleX(1); }
    to   { transform: scaleX(0); }
  }

  /* === Modal mount / unmount animations =================================
     Bound by the host below via the .modal-card-{mount,unmount} and
     .modal-backdrop-{mount,unmount} classes. The card open/close use
     multi-keyframe spring-y curves so the modal POPS in with a small
     overshoot and POPS out with a tiny anticipation windup before
     falling — much more present than a plain ease. Backdrop stays on a
     clean fade (a bouncing backdrop reads as a bug, not polish).

     Durations: the modal-card-out keyframe stays at 220ms to match
     CLOSE_ANIM_MS in studio/modal_host — that's the timeout before the
     entry is spliced off the stack. Bump CLOSE_ANIM_MS if you lengthen
     this.

     Re-skinning: don't fight these classes from an app stylesheet (this
     inline block sits later in the document, so it wins ties). Instead
     register a NEW animation — one entry in window.ModalAnimations plus
     one class in the app's CSS — and open modals with that key. */

  @keyframes modal-card-in {
    0%   { opacity: 0; transform: scale(0.86) translateY(12px); }
    55%  { opacity: 1; transform: scale(1.04) translateY(-3px); } /* overshoot */
    80%  {              transform: scale(0.99) translateY(1px);  } /* small undershoot */
    100% { opacity: 1; transform: scale(1) translateY(0);        } /* settle */
  }
  @keyframes modal-card-out {
    0%   { opacity: 1; transform: scale(1) translateY(0);         }
    25%  { opacity: 1; transform: scale(1.03) translateY(-2px);   } /* windup */
    100% { opacity: 0; transform: scale(0.92) translateY(4px);    } /* fall */
  }
  @keyframes modal-backdrop-in {
    from { opacity: 0; }
    to   { opacity: 1; }
  }
  @keyframes modal-backdrop-out {
    from { opacity: 1; }
    to   { opacity: 0; }
  }

  /* Bounce ease the spring; the keyframes already do most of the work,
     the easing just smooths the segments between them. */
  .modal-card-mount       { animation: modal-card-in     320ms cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.4, 0.5, 1) both; }
  .modal-card-unmount     { animation: modal-card-out    220ms cubic-bezier(0.6, -0.2, 0.7, 0.4) forwards; }
  .modal-backdrop-mount   { animation: modal-backdrop-in 180ms ease-out both; }
  .modal-backdrop-unmount { animation: modal-backdrop-out 200ms ease-in forwards; }

  /* Named entrance animation: "shake" — a "not quite yet" nope. The card
     scales/fades in fast (first ~22%) then does a damped horizontal
     wobble that settles to center, reading as "you can't do that just
     yet." Wired via the ModalAnimations registry (studio/modal_host): open a modal
     with { enterAnim: 'shake' } to use it. Duration here MUST match
     ModalAnimations.enter.shake.ms (600). */
  @keyframes modal-card-shake-in {
    0%   { opacity: 0; transform: scale(0.92) translateX(0);   }
    22%  { opacity: 1; transform: scale(1)    translateX(-11px); }
    37%  {             transform: scale(1)    translateX(9px);   }
    52%  {             transform: scale(1)    translateX(-7px);  }
    67%  {             transform: scale(1)    translateX(5px);   }
    82%  {             transform: scale(1)    translateX(-3px);  }
    100% { opacity: 1; transform: scale(1)    translateX(0);    }
  }
  .modal-card-shake-in { animation: modal-card-shake-in 600ms cubic-bezier(0.36, 0.07, 0.19, 0.97) both; }

  /* Inter-modal swap animations — directional slide for forward-flow feel.
     The leaving modal slides off to the right + fades; the new one enters
     from the left. Pairs with $store.modals.swap() (or open(_, _,
     { replace: true })) — the store flips _swappingOut on the leaving
     entry, waits CLOSE_ANIM_MS, then flips _swappingIn on the entering
     entry. Total swap duration ~440ms (220 out + 220 in). */
  @keyframes modal-card-slide-out-right {
    from { opacity: 1; transform: translateX(0) scale(1); }
    to   { opacity: 0; transform: translateX(48px) scale(0.98); }
  }
  @keyframes modal-card-slide-in-left {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: translateX(-48px) scale(0.98); }
    to   { opacity: 1; transform: translateX(0) scale(1); }
  }
  .modal-card-swap-out { animation: modal-card-slide-out-right 220ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 1, 1) forwards; }
  .modal-card-swap-in  { animation: modal-card-slide-in-left   220ms cubic-bezier(0, 0, 0.2, 1) both; }

  /* Back-direction pair — current step slides off stage-LEFT, next slides
     in from the RIGHT. Mirror of the forward pair above; together they
     give wizard nav a directional language (forward = right, back = left)
     so the user's spatial model of "where the previous step lives" is
     preserved. Trigger: $store.modals.advance(patch, { direction: 'back' })
     or swap(id, props, { direction: 'back' }). */
  @keyframes modal-card-slide-out-left {
    from { opacity: 1; transform: translateX(0) scale(1); }
    to   { opacity: 0; transform: translateX(-48px) scale(0.98); }
  }
  @keyframes modal-card-slide-in-right {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: translateX(48px) scale(0.98); }
    to   { opacity: 1; transform: translateX(0) scale(1); }
  }
  .modal-card-swap-out-back { animation: modal-card-slide-out-left 220ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 1, 1) forwards; }
  .modal-card-swap-in-back  { animation: modal-card-slide-in-right 220ms cubic-bezier(0, 0, 0.2, 1) both; }

  /* Honor reduced-motion preferences — drop the spring/slide motion,
     just fade fast. Vestibular safety. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .modal-card-mount,
    .modal-card-unmount,
    .modal-card-shake-in,
    .modal-card-swap-out,
    .modal-card-swap-in,
    .modal-card-swap-out-back,
    .modal-card-swap-in-back,
    .modal-backdrop-mount,
    .modal-backdrop-unmount {
      animation-duration: 80ms;
      animation-timing-function: linear;
    }
    @keyframes modal-card-shake-in { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }
    @keyframes modal-card-in  { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }
    @keyframes modal-card-out { from { opacity: 1; } to { opacity: 0; } }
    @keyframes modal-card-slide-out-right { from { opacity: 1; } to { opacity: 0; } }
    @keyframes modal-card-slide-in-left   { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }
    @keyframes modal-card-slide-out-left  { from { opacity: 1; } to { opacity: 0; } }
    @keyframes modal-card-slide-in-right  { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }
  }
</style>

<%# THE STORE IS NOT HERE. $store.modals, its focus trap, window.ModalAnimations,
    window.StudioModals (CARD_WIDTHS, DEFAULT_CARD_WIDTH, MIN_LOAD_MS and
    holdAtLeast) and the bfcache / Turbo snapshot cleanup are studio/modal_host,
    bound to this host by the modal-host controller on the template below. The
    store registers before Alpine starts; see that module's header. %>

<template x-if="$store.modals.current()"
          data-studio-controller="modal-host"
          data-modal-host-store-value="modals">
  <%# Backdrop + card use CSS keyframe animations (NOT Alpine
      x-transition), all shipped in the inline style block above. On
      mount, the .modal-backdrop-mount + card mount classes fire fade-in
      + drop-recoil. On close, the store flips _closing, the :class
      bindings add the unmount classes, the exit keyframes play, and
      after the exit duration the store splices the entry from the stack
      and the whole thing unmounts (now invisible).

      A modal can opt out of escape + click-outside dismissal by setting
      dismissible: false on its props (e.g. processing an on-chain tx
      where an accidental click would orphan a signed but un-confirmed
      transaction). Defaults to dismissible. %>
  <%# FOCUS AND NAME. Measured on a real page before this: document.activeElement
      after opening a modal was still the BACKGROUND page's textarea, so a keyboard
      user sat focused behind an overlay. On the non-dismissible cards that is a
      genuine trap — escape and click-outside are deliberately gated off there, so
      there was no way out at all.

      tabindex="-1" makes the backdrop programmatically focusable without adding it
      to the tab order. The name comes from props (ariaLabel, else title, else the
      modal id) so a screen reader never announces a bare unnamed dialog. %>
  <div class="fixed inset-0 z-[var(--z-modal)] flex items-center justify-center p-4 modal-backdrop-mount"
       :class="$store.modals.current()?._closing && 'modal-backdrop-unmount'"
       style="background:rgba(0,0,0,0.6)"
       role="dialog"
       aria-modal="true"
       tabindex="-1"
       x-init="$store.modals.captureFocus($el)"
       :aria-label="$store.modals.dialogLabel()"
       @keydown.tab.prevent="$store.modals.cycleFocus($el, $event)"
       @keydown.escape.window="$store.modals.current() && $store.modals.current().props.dismissible !== false && $store.modals.close()"
       @click.self="$store.modals.current() && $store.modals.current().props.dismissible !== false && $store.modals.close()">
    <%# Card animation is class-driven (cardClasses() in the store) so it
        can pick the right keyframe for the current store state. Plain
        mount/unmount for first open + close, resolved through the
        ModalAnimations registry (props.enterAnim / props.exitAnim).
        Swap-out + swap-in for inter-modal transitions triggered by
        $store.modals.swap() / advance() (or open with { replace: true }).
        _swapDir picks forward (exit right / enter left — default) vs
        back (exit left / enter right — opt-in via { direction: 'back' }).
        The mount class is gated on !_settled so when a swap-in completes
        the class binding does NOT snap back to the mount class and
        re-fire the bounce-in keyframe from opacity:0 — without the gate,
        the card briefly disappears and bounces in again right as the
        slide finishes, which reads as a flash mid-transition. _settled
        stays true for the rest of the entry's lifetime so a stale Alpine
        re-evaluation can't retrigger the bounce. %>
    <%# max-h + overflow-y so a card taller than the viewport SCROLLS instead of
        clipping its own actions off-screen. Without it the confirm button on a long
        card is unreachable at small heights — and on a non-dismissible card that is
        a dead end, since escape and click-outside are gated off. 100dvh, not 100vh:
        mobile browsers shrink the visual viewport when the URL bar is showing, and
        vh ignores that. %>
    <%# No max-w-* here — cardClasses() supplies exactly one, per modal id, from
        the CARD_WIDTHS registry (studio/modal_host). max-h/overflow are unrelated and stay. %>
    <div class="bg-surface rounded-xl border border-subtle shadow-2xl p-6 w-full max-h-[85dvh] overflow-y-auto"
         :class="$store.modals.cardClasses()">
      <%# Consumer-provided content registrations. Each block typically
          contains a <template x-if="$store.modals.current().id === 'X'">
          render "modals/X" </template>.
          NOT `yield if block_given?`. In a partial block_given? is always true,
          and a caller-less yield returns the layout's content, so a host
          rendered from a layout with no block (an app whose modals all live in
          _host_extras) put the whole page body inside the card. See
          Studio::PartialBlock. %>
      <%= Studio::PartialBlock.content(self, yield) %>

      <%# APP-WIDE modal registrations — the second seam. The block above is
          per-CALLSITE: an app rendering this host from two layouts (a live one
          and, say, an /admin preview harness) must repeat every registration in
          both. A modal that belongs to the APP rather than to the page defines
          app/views/modals/_host_extras.html.erb instead and is registered ONCE,
          for every render path through this partial.

          Convention, not a local, on purpose: a local would have to be passed at
          each call site, which is the duplication this exists to remove. An app
          that ships no such partial renders nothing and changes nothing.

          It sits INSIDE the card, beside the block, because each registration is
          a <template x-if> keyed on $store.modals.current().id — the outer
          template x-if above is what guarantees current() is non-null here. %>
      <% if lookup_context.exists?("host_extras", ["modals"], true) %>
        <%= render "modals/host_extras" %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </div>
</template>
